# WHAT MUST NOT GO INTO THE IMAGE. A security control, not a build optimisation.
#
# `COPY . /app` copies the build context verbatim, and on a live machine this tree holds data-bureau.db
# (every workspace's objectives, agent output, audit rows), data-bureau.json, bureau.log, and .git --
# which carries every secret ever committed and later reverted, still reachable by hash.
#
# Image layers are not private: content-addressed, cached, pushed, and readable by anyone who can pull,
# via `docker history` and a tar extract, with no container running. A secret cannot be removed by
# deleting it in a later layer either -- the earlier layer still holds it.
#
# DENY FIRST, then admit, because the two failure modes are not symmetric:
#   - allowlist too narrow -> the image fails to start. Loud, immediate, fixed in minutes.
#   - denylist too narrow  -> workspace data or a credential is baked into a layer. Silent, and possibly
#                             already pushed by the time anyone looks.
# The build is therefore arranged to break rather than to leak.
#
# Derived, not guessed: `git ls-files` for top-level modules plus every local import in server.mjs,
# checked 2026-08-20. server.mjs imports exactly one local module (tools/git-env.mjs) and has no dynamic
# imports, so static reading is sufficient. A new top-level module will fail the next build with
# MODULE_NOT_FOUND naming itself.
*

# --- runtime, admitted deliberately -----------------------------------------------------------------
!package.json
!server.mjs
!public/

# tools/ wholesale rather than just the one imported file (tools/git-env.mjs). First-party code, no
# secrets, and it is what makes the container diagnosable from inside: tools/hunt-log.mjs is precisely
# what someone needs when a scheduled run in a container appears to have done nothing. A container you
# cannot interrogate gets debugged by guesswork.
!tools/

# AGPL-3.0: handing someone an image is conveying the program, and section 4 requires the licence text to
# travel with it. Omitting this is a licence violation, not untidiness.
!LICENSE

# --- re-excluded INSIDE the admitted trees ----------------------------------------------------------
# The directory allowances above are wholesale, so anything sensitive that ever lands inside one needs
# naming here.
**/data/
**/data-dev/
**/bureauProjects/
**/*.db
**/*.db-journal
**/*.db-wal
**/*.log
**/*.log.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/node_modules/
